---
/*
  THE GALLERY. Revised 2026-08-02 from an archive, by the owner's decision, and
  it is a change of kind rather than of degree (DESIGN-PLAN section 18).

  AN ARCHIVE WANTS CHRONOLOGY AND PROVENANCE. Dates, one frame per distinct
  piece, a caption naming what each thing is. Section 18 set a condition on
  exactly that: without years it was a mood board, not a record.

  A GALLERY WANTS SEQUENCE AND RHYTHM. So:

  - THE YEAR IS GONE, from the schema and from every frame. The condition in
    section 18 is withdrawn because it was measuring the wrong object.
  - NAMES ARE OPTIONAL and usually absent. A frame is allowed to be silent, and
    most are: this is imagery, and a label on a photograph that needs none is
    an interruption.
  - RHYTHM REPLACES UNIFORMITY. Frames alternate tall and short rather than
    marching at one height, the same device the Creature index uses, because a
    sequence of identical rectangles is a contact sheet no matter how large the
    rectangles are.
  - CURATION IS LOOSER. The old rule was one frame per distinct piece, which
    existed to stop an archive double-counting. A gallery has nothing to
    double-count: a frame earns its place by being a good photograph.

  Still deliberately not a grid, and still full bleed.
*/
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import MediaSurface from "../../components/MediaSurface.astro";
import TextSurface from "../../components/TextSurface.astro";
import {getGallery, isPlaceholderText} from "../../lib/content";
import {LOCALES, toLocale} from "../../lib/locales";
import {imageUrl} from "../../lib/image";
import {overlayTheme} from "../../lib/media";
import {t} from "../../i18n/ui";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return LOCALES.map((lang) => ({params: {lang}}));
}

const locale = toLocale(Astro.params.lang);
const images = (await getGallery()).filter((image) => image.media?.length);
const first = images[0]?.media?.[0] ?? null;

/* Flattened first, so the rhythm counts FRAMES and not documents. */
const frames = images.flatMap((image) =>
  (image.media ?? []).map((media, index) => ({media, title: index === 0 ? image.title : null})),
);

/*
  PORTRAIT FRAMES PAIR AT DESKTOP (2026-08-03, after looking at the page at
  1440). A single full-bleed frame is 1440x790 there, which is a 1.8:1
  letterbox, and eleven of these twelve photographs are 3:4 portraits. The page
  was showing horizontal bands cut out of the middle of the owner's photographs:
  the fur frame stopped reading as an object at all. On a phone the same frames
  are correct, which is why it survived this long.

  So the SHAPE OF THE PHOTOGRAPH decides, not its position: a landscape frame
  runs the full width, portraits go two across and touching. That is what the
  primary reference does at desktop, measured in section 14 (one 1440x900, then
  pairs of 720x900 side by side, no gutter), and it is why the Creature index
  already looks right at this width.

  It keeps the gallery distinct from that index, which is the risk section 23
  flag 5 names: the index is a uniform wall of pairs, and here the one landscape
  frame breaks the row, which is the rhythm this page is for.

  On a phone nothing changes: one column, alternating tall and short.
*/
const isLandscape = (media: (typeof frames)[number]["media"]) => {
  const d = media.dimensions;
  return Boolean(d && d.width > d.height);
};
---

<Base
  locale={locale}
  title={t(locale, "gallery")}
  chrome={overlayTheme(first?.overlay)}
  image={first ? imageUrl(first.poster, 1200) : null}
  theme="dark"
  footerTheme="dark"
>
  <h1 class="sr-only">{t(locale, "gallery")}</h1>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2">
    {frames.map(({media, title}, index) => (
      <div class:list={["contents md:block", isLandscape(media) && "md:col-span-2"]}>
        <MediaSurface
          media={media}
          locale={locale}
          height={index % 2 === 0 ? "tall" : "short"}
          desktopHeight="tall"
          priority={index === 0}
        >
          {/*
            SILENT unless the frame has a real name. Section 41 says so outright:
            in a gallery the name is optional and usually absent, the frame is
            silent. Every frame was printing {NOME_PEZZO}, in Italian, on the
            English page too, which is scaffolding laid over the photography.
          */}
          {title && !isPlaceholderText(title) && (
            <h2 class="type-label text-label">{title}</h2>
          )}
        </MediaSurface>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  {
    frames.length === 0 && (
      <TextSurface theme="dark" class="flex min-h-svh flex-col justify-center">
        <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{"{GALLERY_EMPTY}"}</p>
      </TextSurface>
    )
  }
</Base>
